INT. PARKING GARAGE - LEVEL 3 - NIGHT

Concrete pillars. Fluorescent tubes buzzing. A rented limousine idling with its doors open, bass pulsing faintly from a dance hall a block away.

FRANKIE DELACORTE (17, rented tuxedo two sizes large, corsage crushed in his fist) sits on the limo bumper. MALIK OYELARAN (17, his best friend, bow tie hanging) leans against a pillar, spinning a set of car keys on one finger. BLYTHE ARMSTRONG-LEE (17, Frankie's date, silver dress, heels in one hand) stands at the edge of the headlights, arms folded.

Nobody speaks for a long while.

MALIK
Well. That was a dance.

FRANKIE
Don't.

MALIK
I'm only saying.

BLYTHE
He's only saying.

FRANKIE
Blythe.

BLYTHE
You said you'd tell him.

FRANKIE
I was going to.

BLYTHE
You said, quote, "Tonight."

Malik stops spinning the keys. He looks from one to the other, then at the floor. Frankie stands, brushes his knees, and takes a step toward Blythe; she steps back; Malik takes a step forward as if to separate them; and all three of them stop at once, caught in the light like three deer on a road.

MALIK
Tell me what?

FRANKIE
Nothing.

BLYTHE ^
That he's moving to Denver.

MALIK
(to Frankie)
You told her first.

FRANKIE
Mal.

MALIK
You told her before me.

FRANKIE
It was an accident.

MALIK
How do you accidentally tell someone you're leaving?

BLYTHE
He doesn't leave till August.

MALIK
August is three months.

BLYTHE
Three months is a long time.

MALIK
It's the length of a summer, Blythe.

FRANKIE
Hey. I'm standing right here.

MALIK
Then say something.

Frankie opens his mouth. Closes it. His corsage drops to the concrete. Malik shoves him, once, in the chest, not hard. Frankie stumbles back against the limo. Neither moves.

Blythe sets her heels on the hood and wraps her arms around herself. A car alarm chirps somewhere on the level below.

FRANKIE
I didn't want it to end like this.

MALIK
It's not ending. It's Denver.

FRANKIE
It's the same thing.

MALIK
It is not.

He picks up the corsage. He hands it back. Frankie turns it over in his hand, then holds it out to Blythe, who looks at it, takes it, and pins it to Malik's lapel.

BLYTHE
There. Now we all have a flower.

MALIK
I'm not wearing this.

BLYTHE
You are.

He lets it stay.
